detailed:
Per-turn async repair is the right direction, but not as an unbounded `Promise.all` over all dirty turns. A per-turn repair unit (`repairTurnMaintenance(threadId, turnId, deps)`) should handle smooth turn data, lower-band projection, raw/smooth token counts, and status reporting, but production maintenance needs bounded concurrency or sequential/batched writes because:

- `turns.json` writes will race on revisions if many turns repair in parallel;
- provider/API calls for token counting/projection can spike;
- chunk repair depends on repaired turn projections/smooth text, so ordering matters.

Recommended shape: cheaply scan dirty turns, repair a bounded batch, batch-write turn updates where possible, update chunks once, then repair selected chunks/bands with limited concurrency, and persist remaining backlog/status. For production `turn_end`, keep the work small/time-bounded; for a manual repair command, full dirty repair is fine as long as it is still concurrency-limited.

This also exposed the limits of the current file-backed canonical store. A DB-backed canonical store would better support selective row updates, transactional multi-record repair, background workers, claimable repair jobs, and incremental progress tracking. The ideal DB model would include tables like `turns`, `messages`, `chunks`, `artifacts`, `repair_jobs`, and `token_counts`. For now, stay conservative with the file store: bounded sequential/batched repair, manual full repair command, clear reporting, and no parallel write storms.

On JSONL/file behavior: PI session JSONL can be loaded in file order/parent-chain order as generated; it does not need in-place line updates. Canonical `messages.jsonl` is append-only with explicit `sourceOrder`/`sourceRevision`, and readers sort/use source order as needed. Smart compact reads canonical thread/messages/turns/chunks and builds a fresh projection rather than mutating JSONL lines in place. Functionally this is fine, but it becomes inefficient at scale because it requires full-file reads/parses, full rewrites of `turns.json`/`chunks.json`, repeated open-read-write cycles, sequential provider/counting work, and coarse revision conflicts.

